Curves $A$ and $B$ both pass through the point where $t=0$ and $Q=40$.
One satisfies $\dfrac{dQ}{dt}=0.3Q$ and the other satisfies $\dfrac{dQ}{dt}=-0.3Q$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$t$}, ylabel={$Q$}, xmin=0, xmax=6, ymin=0, ymax=210, xtick={1,2,3,4,5}, ytick={40,80,120,160,200}, samples=100, clip=false, ylabel near ticks]
\addplot[thick, red, domain=0:5] {40*exp(0.3*x)} node[pos=0.85, left] {$A$};
\addplot[thick, blue, dashed, domain=0:5] {40*exp(-0.3*x)} node[pos=0.8, above right] {$B$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
State which curve has $k>0$, and give a reason.
